  "problem": "In a certain school, $17.5%$ of students are left-handed. In a random sample of $8$ students from this school, what is the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of left-handed students in the group.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ students in the group\n\n- the probability of a student being left-handed is $p=0.175$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\u223cB(8,0.175).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.175)}^{x}{(1-0.175)}^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.175)}^{x}{(0.825)}^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =P(X=6)+P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{6}){(0.175)}^{6}{(0.825)}^{8-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7}){(0.175)}^{7}{(0.825)}^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8}){(0.175)}^{8}{(0.825)}^{8-8} \\\\  & =28{(0.175)}^{6}{(0.825)}^{2}+8{(0.175)}^{7}{(0.825)}^{1}+1{(0.175)}^{8}{(0.825)}^{0} \\\\  & \u22480.0006\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.",
